+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits
+
+## Context
+
+Twenty-four seasonal field workers share twelve tablets to survey 320 permanent plots, producing about 6,000 plot-visit records a season. Tablets can be offline for 48 hours, and workers sometimes correct the same visit independently after comparing field notes in the evening. Two edits of one visit can therefore both be derived from the same starting state and arrive days apart, in either order.
+
+The record store has to say what the current value of such a visit is. The answer governs more than storage: it fixes what a tablet must retain and send, what review work a coordinator is presented with, and which visits can enter the reviewed CSV the regional science partner receives every Friday.
+
+What is at stake if it goes wrong is the scientific record. A visit's fields are not independent in the way their names suggest — a species identification and its cover estimate move together, and an observation date can change what an observer list means. Getting this wrong does not produce a visible error; it produces a plausible record that no observer submitted, exported to a science partner who cannot tell.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+One inherited gate eliminates an option in the domain:
+
+- **No submitted edit is discarded silently** *(inherited; HLD tenet)*. A mechanism that lets one worker's accepted observations disappear without a person deciding so fails outright.
+
+Every option preserves the original submissions and their authors in storage, and none lets arrival order decide which submission is authoritative; those are background rather than decision elements.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Integrity of the stored record — major.** Whether a stored current value is one that an observer submitted or a coordinator composed, rather than one the system assembled. Turns on the project's non-goal of inferring scientific intent, and on the tenet that the path from a submitted observation to an exported value stays inspectable.
+- **Ongoing maintenance burden — major.** What has to be understood and kept correct over seasons, by a team with no dedicated systems administrator (HLD tenet).
+- **Coordinator review workload — moderate.** How many of roughly 6,000 seasonal visits reach two coordinators as review work, against a weekly export rhythm.
+- **Export readiness — moderate.** How many visits are in an exportable state by Friday, per the goal of making reviewable problems visible before the export.
+- **Explainability of an exported value — moderate.** What a coordinator can reconstruct, months later, about why an exported cell holds what it holds.
+- **Worker-visible correction latency — moderate.** How long a worker's correction waits between submission and appearing in the record. This criterion cuts against the selected option and is recorded for that reason.
+- **Tablet submission and storage cost — minor.** What each tablet retains per draft and sends per submission across a 48-hour disconnection.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Every submission carries the complete visit payload plus the ID of the revision it was edited from. The server stores it as an immutable revision in a per-visit graph. A submission edited from the current revision extends it. A submission edited from anything else becomes a competing head, and the visit has no current value until a coordinator resolves it by adopting one head's payload or composing a payload from the competing ones. Resolution is recorded as a revision naming every head it collapses, so the losing submissions stay reachable with their original authors.
+
+**Demonstration.** Two workers both fetch revision `r4`. One corrects an observer's name, the other adds a missing species. Both submissions name base `r4`, both are accepted, and the visit now has two heads. It is held out of Friday's export until a coordinator reads both and submits the payload that should stand — even though the two edits touch no common field.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **No submitted edit discarded silently** *(gate)*: passes. Nothing is overwritten, and the only way a submission stops being the current value is a coordinator's recorded decision.
+- **Integrity of the stored record** *(major)*: strong. Every current value is a payload some person submitted whole — a worker's or a coordinator's. The server composes nothing.
+- **Ongoing maintenance burden** *(major)*: strong. The rules are base-pointer bookkeeping and a head count; there is no per-field policy to keep correct as the survey form changes.
+- **Coordinator review workload** *(moderate)*: weak. Any pair of concurrent edits to a visit produces review work, including edits to unrelated fields and edits whose payloads agree.
+- **Export readiness** *(moderate)*: partial. Diverged visits are unexportable until a person acts, so the Friday set depends on the queue being worked. Divergence is visible immediately rather than discovered at export time.
+- **Explainability of an exported value** *(moderate)*: strong. An exported value traces to one revision, and the resolution above it names its contributing submissions and the coordinator who chose.
+- **Worker-visible correction latency** *(moderate)*: weak. A trivial correction that collides with any other edit waits for coordinator attention.
+- **Tablet submission and storage cost** *(minor)*: strong. A draft retains one revision ID beside its payload; a submission is one payload.
+
+**Summary.** Trades coordinator time for a record the system never assembles.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each tablet retains the base state it fetched and submits structured changes against it. The server combines changes that touch disjoint fields automatically, so the two workers in the example above both see their correction land with no review. Only overlapping changes — the same field changed two ways — are routed to a coordinator. Merging is governed by per-field-kind rules: scalar fields take the single changed side, observer lists merge as sets, and species entries merge per species key with cover estimates treated as scalars within each entry. Field-level provenance is retained so each field of a combined record names the submission it came from. This option does not require discarding anything, and it is compatible with holding every automatically combined record for manual approval before export.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **No submitted edit discarded silently** *(gate)*: passes. Disjoint changes are all retained, and overlaps go to a person.
+- **Integrity of the stored record** *(major)*: weak. Disjointness is computed over field names, and the fields carry scientific relationships the rules cannot see — a revised identification merged with the other worker's cover estimate for the species as previously identified yields a record neither submitted and neither would endorse. The failure is silent and individually plausible.
+- **Ongoing maintenance burden** *(major)*: weak. The rules are a standing artifact that must be re-reasoned whenever the survey form gains a field, and their failures are not the kind a test suite written against today's form would catch.
+- **Coordinator review workload** *(moderate)*: strong. Only genuine same-field disagreements reach a coordinator, which is a small fraction of concurrent edits.
+- **Export readiness** *(moderate)*: strong. Most concurrently edited visits are exportable with no human action.
+- **Explainability of an exported value** *(moderate)*: partial. Provenance is finer-grained than a revision, but an exported row may have no single author, and reconstructing why a cell holds its value means reading the merge rules as they stood at merge time.
+- **Worker-visible correction latency** *(moderate)*: strong. Non-overlapping corrections land on submission.
+- **Tablet submission and storage cost** *(minor)*: partial. Each draft retains a base snapshot as well as its edits, and submissions carry a change structure.
+
+**Summary.** Trades a class of silent, scientifically wrong records for most of the review work.
+
+### Arrival-order current state
+
+**Description.** The newest accepted submission is the visit's current value. Earlier submissions are retained in history for audit, but no review work is created and no coordinator decides.
+
+**Analysis.**
+
+- **No submitted edit discarded silently** *(gate)*: eliminated. A correction stops being the record because another submission arrived later, with no person deciding and nothing shown to either worker. This is the behavior the project exists to replace.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.**
+
+The decision turns on the two major criteria, which point the same way. On integrity of the stored record, revisions are strong and merge is weak, and the weakness is the specific one this domain cannot absorb: an automatically combined record is wrong invisibly, in a dataset whose consumer is a science partner with no way to audit it. On maintenance burden, a team with no dedicated systems administrator would own a per-field merge policy indefinitely — the HLD's tenet about workflows two coordinators can operate reads directly against that. Merge wins three moderate criteria and the minor one, and that is the honest cost of this selection rather than a reason to reconsider it: review workload, export readiness, and correction latency are all paid in coordinator hours, and coordinator hours are recoverable in a way a silently fabricated observation is not.
+
+The decision is taken *for* two tenets — do not silently discard a submitted edit, and keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable — and *against* none. The non-goal of inferring scientific intent is what makes the merge option's field-name disjointness insufficient rather than merely imperfect.
+
+**What this forecloses.** The server never combines two submissions on its own. Unrelated edits to one visit produce review work, and a visit with unresolved review work has no current value, so it cannot reach an export by default. Expect the queue to carry visits whose competing payloads agree or differ only in untouched fields; that is accepted, not a defect to optimize away silently.
+
+**Why the other approach stays worth considering.** Merge is not refuted — it is outscored on criteria whose weights come from this team's staffing and this dataset's consumer. Three changes would make it live again: review volume proving unsustainable for two coordinators at season peak; a survey form whose fields are genuinely independent, or a stated set of fields with no scientific relationship to any other (observer names and free-text notes are the candidates) for which merging is safe by construction; or a scientific reviewer joining the team who can own merge rules as a maintained artifact. The narrow form — merging a declared safe-field set and diverging on everything else — is the version to revisit first, because it keeps this decision's integrity property for the fields that carry science. Revisiting means re-running the criteria above, not restarting the deliberation.
+
+**What this obligates downstream.** Both dependent nodes are sized around this contract and define no concurrency semantics of their own.
+
+- **tablet-sync** retains the base revision ID beside every local draft across restarts and multi-day disconnection, sends it in each submission, and distinguishes the acceptance outcomes for the worker — the edit extended the current revision, or it became a competing revision pending coordinator review. A worker who edits a visit that is already diverged is adding another head, and the interface has to be able to say so. Rejection of a submission whose base the server does not recognize is a case the tablet handles rather than retries blindly.
+- **review-export** needs a comparison view over a diverged visit's competing heads and their divergence point, a resolution action that carries every current head, and an export set restricted to settled visits. Because resolution adopts or composes a whole payload, the comparison is between complete visits, not between fields. The coordinator's resolution is itself a recorded authorship event and appears in the export's provenance.
